# How humans and agents work together on {{ project_name }}

This is the rationale document for the coordination process — the *why* behind the terse conventions in `AGENTS.md` (CLAUDE.md imports it), which agents follow mechanically. Read this once as a human joining the project; agents don't need it. The pattern was developed and piloted on `UABGH-Emerging-Technologies/lead-infinitely` (2026-07); canonical home: the `coordination/` template in `UABPeriopAI/Templates`.

## The premise

Coding agents made writing code cheap. What they did not make cheap: deciding what to build, reviewing what got built, and the human sign-offs that gate real-world use. So the scarce resource is **human judgment**, and the process is designed as queue management for it:

- **Agents enqueue judgment requests** (a PR to review, a packet to approve, a decision to make) as cards.
- **Humans drain the queue in batches**, on their schedule — the board is a mailbox, not a pager.
- Everything else (what the code does, why decisions were made, how to cold-start) lives in the repo's markdown files, which agents read fluently and keep current.

The split that makes this work: **the board holds coordination state; the md files hold knowledge.** A card is a title, a state, an owner, and a link. If you're writing paragraphs into a card, stop — that content belongs in a notes file, a HUMAN-REVIEW-QUEUE entry, or the PR, with the card linking to it.

## The board

One shared board per project (not per developer — the whole point is that all open human gates are visible in one place). Personal views come from filtering by assignee, not from separate boards.

### Columns, precisely

| Column | Meaning | How a card leaves |
|---|---|---|
| **Unshaped** | The fluid project plan: everything we believe we'll do, as cheap one-line **draft items** (board-only, not repo issues). Carries source + gate only. | Someone *shapes* it: writes a self-contained description, converts draft → real issue, adds labels/assignee/Responsible, moves to Todo. |
| **Todo** | Shaped and ready to execute — an agent (or human) could pick this up cold. | Work starts → Agent working (or the human just does it). |
| **Agent working** | A coding agent is actively executing. Draft PRs live here too — a draft PR means the agent isn't done, not that review is owed. | PR opens for review → Awaiting code review. Abandoned or paused without a PR → swept back to Todo (see gotchas). |
| **Awaiting code review** | A diff exists; the asked-for act is code review + merge. | Clean review → merge → Done. |
| **Awaiting human action** | No diff. A decision, sign-off, or real-world act. | The human acts: comment carries the substance; **close = decision complete** (approve or reject). A change-request comment instead bounces the card to Agent working and back — see signals vs payload. |
| **Done** | Merged / approved / verified. Closed issues are ground truth, so this column is real, not decorative. | — |

**There is deliberately no Blocked column.** "Blocked" answers *nobody acts next*, which is how columns become graveyards. Blocked-on-a-human **is** Awaiting human action. Blocked-on-other-work stays in Unshaped (or Todo) with its gate named in the body. Every column answers "who acts next" — that's the invariant.

**Unshaped is Shape Up vocabulary on purpose.** Converting a draft item into a real issue *is* the shaping act: writing a description so self-contained that an agent (or a stakeholder reading a relayed packet) can act without the conversation that produced it. Cheap code made this more important, not less — agents exploit ambiguity worse than humans do. (Comments added to a card later are part of its spec: agents read them at pickup, and surface — never guess — when a comment contradicts the body.)

**Who shapes: shaping is human-triggered; agents draft, humans ratify.** Promoting drafts to Todo is choosing what the team works on next — a bet, not a lookup — so it never happens without a human asking. Default mode: a human says "shape the next block," the agent mines the plan docs and writes the cards, the human ratifies by skimming (editing or demoting a card is cheap). For contested bets, talk it through first and file the cards as the outcome; and nothing stops a human shaping by hand in the UI. An agent may *offer* to shape when the board runs dry — the promotion waits for a yes.

### Card anatomy

- **Title**: the ask, imperative, glanceable.
- **Status**: the column.
- **Assignee** (GitHub account): the *accountable human*. **Every card gets one — assigning the wrong human beats assigning no one.** With multiple developers, the assignee also partitions the board into lanes: *an agent starts work only on cards assigned to its own operator.* Bookkeeping and cleared-gate processing are deliberately **not** lane-scoped (global, idempotent-or-claimed — see `process/next.md`): otherwise a cleared gate's follow-on work would wait not for a human decision but for *a particular human to open a session*, the anti-pattern this design exists to avoid.
- **Responsible** (custom single-select): who *actually acts* — including actors GitHub can't represent: `AI agent` (the coding agent, whichever tool the developer runs), each developer, each stakeholder, `Copilot (AI reviewer)`.
- **Labels**: `human-review`, `agent-work`, `stakeholder` (routes the card to relay delivery for non-GitHub stakeholders).
- **Body**: the self-contained ask + link(s) to the substance. Nothing else. **The ask goes on the first line** — see below.

**Consolidated gate cards.** Sometimes one human decision clears several gates at once, and carding them separately just makes the human read the same context five times. When that happens, they collapse to one card with checkboxes, and a note at the top of `HUMAN-REVIEW-QUEUE.md` names the consolidation. Two questions, in order. First, **which kind of card is this — does a human *decide* it, or does an agent *do* it?** Then, among gates only: **would one successful review clear all of these?** If yes, one card. If no, they were never the same gate.

This consolidates **gates, not work.** A build card is a different kind of object: it names executable work, it is claimed and worked by an agent, and no amount of reviewing clears it. Distinct build work always gets its own card, on a consolidated topic as much as anywhere else. The pilot's first version of this rule missed that distinction and read as "stop making cards about this topic," which made the next agent hesitate to card real work.

### Lead with the ask

A card exists to hand one decision to a person who was not in the conversation that produced it. Written the other way round — context first, question somewhere in the middle — it costs that person the whole read before they can tell whether it is even theirs to answer.

So the rule is about **order**, not length:

- **First line of a new card's body: the ask itself.** The question or action, in words that need no repo context. Everything else after it.
- **Last comment on a card: same.** Its first line says what is being asked right now — including "nothing right now, this is FYI." If a comment changes the ask, the new ask goes on top.
- **Context after the ask is welcome.** Reasoning, trade-offs and evidence are the point of a comment; they are just not the opening.

The failure mode this replaces: a thread whose current ask can only be reconstructed by reading every comment in order, thesis-last, like an essay that never states its point until the conclusion. A side benefit: with the ask on a predictable line, the orientation report can extract it mechanically instead of guessing from the title.

**And anything a human must read to clear a gate is committed to the repo** — packets, transcripts, rendered artifacts, linked from the card by permalink. "Regenerate it with this script" strands the reviewer on whichever machine produced it, and makes the approved thing unrecoverable later.

### Signals vs payload

A card *move* is the signal; the *substance* travels as an issue comment. "Approve with this caveat" = comment with the caveat, then close. Agents read the comments whenever they see a state change. A closed `human-review` issue with no contrary comment means approved. Relayed stakeholder decisions become exactly comment (with attribution: "Approved by 〈name〉, relayed by 〈assignee〉: …") + close — indistinguishable from a GitHub-native approval.

Humans also deliver payload *without* closing — often correctly: **close is reserved for "my decision on this gate is complete."** Feedback that implies more work is the *middle* of a gate. The `next` procedure scans open gates for unacknowledged human comments: an explicit complete decision is processed and the agent restores the missing signal (closes with attribution); a **change request re-queues the gate rather than auto-starting it**: the gate's processing claim is marked superseded, an `Agent:` acknowledgment is posted, the card moves to Todo with Responsible = AI agent, and the next session's normal lowest-issue-number selection decides whether it is the card that gets worked — it is never automatic. When it is picked, the card oscillates to Agent working while the agent revises, then back to Awaiting human action with a reply saying what changed (one card, N rounds, same semantics as change-requests on a PR); a question gets answered; anything ambiguous is surfaced, never guessed at. The asymmetry is deliberate — an agent may repair a missing close on an unambiguous decision, but never manufacture a decision from a partial one.

## The actors

- **Developers** shape work, review diffs, and drain their judgment queue in batches — the "Awaiting" columns are the batching buffer.
- **Agent sessions** (any coding agent — Claude Code, OpenCode, Codex, …; the protocol is tool-neutral, only entry points differ) file cards, move their own cards truthfully, and orient with the `next` procedure when — and only when — their human invokes it: sync cards to ground truth, process cleared gates, list the human's queue, then pick up the next Todo card in their operator's lane **and work it through** — the session's deliverable is a PR, a merge, or a recorded blocker, never the orientation report on its own. The read-heavy passes run as a script, `process/kanban_check.sh` (spec and by-hand fallback: `process/kanban-check.md`) — read-only, so it cannot loop or mutate; interpreting human payloads and every board write stay with the session. Originally these passes were delegated to a free local agent, on the finding that orientation cost is board-reading rather than judgment (measured on the pilot, 2026-07-29); a year's worth of runs later the delegated ones were also inventing issue numbers, reporting closed issues as open, and writing files into the checkout, so the passes became a script — same saving, and findings that are joins and regexes rather than recollections. Agents are not daemons: when no session runs, moves queue harmlessly.
- **Non-GitHub stakeholders** get `stakeholder`-labeled cards by **relay**: the assignee shows them the packet (email, meeting), then records the decision on the issue.
- **Reviewers**: Copilot code review on every PR push, with the **context-blind fresh-agent fallback** when Copilot is unavailable — a fresh agent, any strong model, prompted essentially only "Review PR #n," deliberately denied the authoring conversation. Fresh eyes catch the author's own staleness; never make one reviewer a hard dependency.

## Known gotchas (learned on the pilot, 2026-07 → 2026-09)

- **Merged-but-not-pulled is invisible to sessions.** Commands/skills and conventions land on origin/main via PRs, but a session only sees its working tree — and tools discover commands at session start. Symptom: a reopened session doesn't offer `/next`. Fix: ff-pull, restart once. `process/next.md` step 0 checks for this.
- Editing a Projects v2 single-select field's options (rename **or** add) **regenerates every option ID and silently clears every card's value** — snapshot `item-list` first, re-apply after, then re-wire the board's built-in workflows (Item closed → Done etc.) in the UI.
- **`gh project item-list` truncates silently, and no fixed limit stays big enough.** The default is 30; `--limit 100` was the fix until the pilot's board outgrew *that*, and the only symptom was a freshly-created card that appeared not to exist. So raising the number is not the lesson: filter server-side (`--query "-status:Done"`, which shrinks `totalCount` itself) and guard every **board** fetch by comparing `totalCount` against the items returned. That qualifier matters — `gh issue list` has no `totalCount` at all (asking for it errors), so the closed-gate and PR fetches need the weaker returned-equals-limit tripwire instead. Exact commands in `process/next.md` step 0. *(This bullet was wrong twice on the pilot before it was right: one PR corrected the advice in four other files and missed this one, a second fixed it here as a drive-by, and a third reverted that second PR wholesale at the owner's request — restoring the bad wording. A revert is scoped to a PR, not to a topic, so an unrelated drive-by fix inside it dies with it. Land a correction on its own card.)*
- **A card can get parked in Agent working, and no future session will touch it.** Starting work deliberately skips Agent-working cards — a live claim reads as "another session has this" — so an agent that stops *without* finishing leaves a card no one picks up and no bookkeeping rule repairs. The pilot found this with a card parked four days: the session holding it had correctly refused to overwrite an uncommitted implementation sitting in another worktree, left an honest handoff note, and waited for a handoff that never came. The pause was right; leaving the card in Agent working was the bug — a parked card and an abandoned one look identical from outside. `next`'s sync pass now sweeps them: newest agent marker older than 24 h, no PR, no branch → claim superseded, findings comment, card back to Todo. A draft PR exempts the card (the agent got somewhere). Nothing is ever deleted, and any partial work that might survive elsewhere gets named in the comment so the next picker recovers it rather than writing a second version.
- **The sharpest version of that is stopping the moment the claim lands.** The session posts its marker, moves the card, reports its orientation, and ends — having produced nothing, while the board now says the card is being worked. It got worse when the orientation passes became a script, because the session no longer arrives at the pick with any momentum behind it, and a clean claim plus a tidy report reads like a finished answer. `next` step 5 states the deliverable outright rather than leaving it to a trailing clause.
- **Two sessions running `next` at once can race on the same card**: Projects v2 has no atomic test-and-set. Arbitration rides the issue comment stream (append-only, ordered by comment ID): claim with a nonce-stamped comment, re-fetch, lowest ID wins, loser marks itself superseded. Protocol in `process/next.md`.
- The Actions `GITHUB_TOKEN` cannot touch Projects v2; board mutations happen from local sessions via `gh` (token needs the `project` scope).
- Markdown edits during board reconciliation go through a temporary worktree + PR, never the launch working tree (a parallel session's `commit -a` can sweep dirty files).

